Founder & Social Copy — Application Spec Samples
Project: Spec work (written to demonstrate skill for job applications, not client work)
Goal: Show founder-led content across platforms — LinkedIn, X/Twitter, Instagram — in a direct-response, DTC/Google Ads voice
Tone: Operator perspective, contrarian-but-grounded, no fluff

These are original spec pieces written to demonstrate voice, research-led thinking, and the ability to adapt one idea across platforms. They are not client work and not a claim of prior ghostwriting experience.

## LinkedIn Post — Thought Leadership

**Most ecommerce brands don't have a traffic problem.**

They have a "give me more traffic" problem.

When sales slow down, the reflex is predictable: increase spend, test another audience, change the campaign, blame the algorithm.

Sometimes the account is fine.

The offer isn't.

Google can put your product in front of someone who is already looking for it. It can't make a weak reason to buy suddenly feel compelling.

So before I touch bids or budgets, I want to know:

Would I stop for this ad?
Would I understand the offer in five seconds?
Would I know why this product instead of the ten sitting next to it?

If the answer is no, another 20% in ad spend isn't a growth strategy.

It's a more expensive way to find the same problem.

## X / Twitter Thread — Operator Perspective

01/08 Your Google Ads account can be healthy and still lose money.

02/08 Good CTR. Clean structure. Plenty of conversions. Nothing obviously broken.

03/08 And the business is still asking, "Where did the margin go?"

04/08 The mistake is treating the ad account like the business.

05/08 Ads can optimize for the conversion you give them. They can't decide whether that conversion is actually valuable.

06/08 If your best-selling product has the worst margin, scaling it harder doesn't make the problem disappear. It scales the problem.

07/08 The better question isn't "How do we get more conversions?"

08/08 It's "Which customers, products and offers are worth buying more of — and can the account see the difference?"

## Instagram Video Script — 45–60 sec

| Beat | Line |
|---|---|
| Hook | If your Google Ads agency starts the meeting with CTR, ask them one question: "So what?" |
| Beat 1 | CTR is useful. I look at it too. But a click isn't the goal. Revenue is. Profit is. Growth you can actually keep is. |
| Beat 2 | You can have a beautiful dashboard full of green numbers while the business underneath it is getting worse. |
| Beat 3 | So when someone tells you, "CTR is up 18%," don't celebrate yet. |
| Payoff | Ask: "Did the extra clicks make us more money?" That's the number I'd rather build a strategy around. |
| Close | Optimize the account, sure. Just don't forget you're running a business, not a spreadsheet. |

## Why These Work

- **Operator voice, not marketer voice** — reads like someone who runs accounts, not someone selling a service
- **One idea, three formats** — the same underlying argument (metrics vs. money) reshaped for LinkedIn's long-form, Twitter's thread cadence, and IG's spoken script
- **Direct response instinct** — opens with a claim that stops the scroll, ends with a reframe the reader can act on
- **No AI-generic tells** — short declarative sentences, no "in today's fast-paced world," no listicle padding

Written to sound like a founder who has opinions, not a content calendar.
